const html: string = "<h2>Eposter Abstracts <span class=\"sp-count\">22</span></h2><p class=\"sp-note\">Submissions are grouped by presentation type. Each entry includes its abstract title, author names, institution, and abstract body.</p><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"eposter-1\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Eposter Abstract 1</p><h3>Adrenal Histoplasmosis Masquerading as a Adrenal Mass: A Rare Diagnostic Mimic of Malignancy</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr B S Aulakh , Dr Amit Tuli, Dr Shikha Tiwari</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Aykai Hospital</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Title: Adrenal Histoplasmosis Masquerading as a Adrenal<br/>\nMass: A Rare Diagnostic Mimic of Malignancy</p><p>Introduction:<br/>\nHistoplasmosis, caused by Histoplasma capsulatum, is an endemic systemic fungal infection that can occasionally involve the adrenal glands. Adrenal involvement is uncommon and may present as a mass lesion mimicking malignancy. When the lesion is closely related to the kidney, diagnostic uncertainty may lead to consideration of renal neoplasia and potentially unnecessary surgical intervention. We report a case of adrenal histoplasmosis presenting as a radiologically suspicious renal mass.</p><p>Case Report:<br/>\nA patient presented with constitutional symptoms and was found to have a renal-adrenal mass on cross-sectional imaging. Contrast-enhanced imaging demonstrated a suspicious mass at the renal–adrenal interface, raising the possibility of an adrenal malignancy. The lesion’s anatomical relationship with the kidney and adrenal gland posed a diagnostic challenge. Renal function and relevant biochemical investigations were assessed, and an appropriate endocrine evaluation was performed to exclude a functioning adrenal lesion. Given the atypical clinical and radiological presentation, tissue diagnosis was pursued. Histopathological examination demonstrated granulomatous inflammation with fungal elements morphologically consistent with Histoplasma capsulatum. The diagnosis of adrenal histoplasmosis was subsequently established, avoiding radical oncological surgery.</p><p>The patient was initiated on systemic antifungal therapy with Voriconazole . The patient demonstrated progressive clinical improvement with subsequent radiological reduction in the size of the mass on follow-up imaging. No evidence of progressive renal involvement was observed.</p><p>Discussion:<br/>\nAdrenal histoplasmosis is an important but under-recognized differential diagnosis of adrenal and renal-adjacent masses, particularly in endemic regions. Its radiological appearance can closely resemble malignancy, tuberculosis and other granulomatous diseases. The presence of constitutional symptoms and atypical imaging findings should prompt consideration of infective etiologies. Tissue diagnosis can be pivotal in distinguishing fungal infection from malignancy and may prevent unnecessary radical surgery.</p><p>Conclusion:<br/>\nAdrenal histoplasmosis can closely mimic a adrenal malignancy and represents an important diagnostic pitfall. A high index of suspicion, accurate anatomical localization and timely tissue diagnosis are essential for appropriate management. Recognition of this rare entity allows potentially curative medical therapy and avoidance of unnecessary major surgery.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"eposter-2\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Eposter Abstract 2</p><h3>Young female with Rt adrenal mass with MEN2A case</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr.Rahul Parmar</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">SMS MEDICAL COLLEGE</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>25 year old female presented to SMS Urology OPD with chief complaints of Recurrent episodes of palpitations, anxiety, sensation of increase body heat and Recurrent episode of headache 2-3 times in day for past 3 months. There is no any Urinary complaints or focal neurological deficits,but there is hyperpigmented macules over upper back,neck and upper chest since 3 months, on blood investigations, there is incraese PTH and normal serum calcium leval suggestive of primary Hyperparathyroidism.History of FNAC of left thyroid nodule 1 month back,suggestive of medullary carcinoma of thyroid Bethesda 5,metanephrine and normetanephrine leval above 4000 suggestive of active metabolic lesion in adrenal.there is no any Surgical History but patient taken hypertensive medications for uncontrolled HTN.there is no history of addiction.<br/>\nRt.Lap.Adrenelectomy done ,intra op- large approx 6*5 cm mass identified at Rt suprarenal region adhrent to rt kidney and post op biopsy report suggestive of pheochromocytoma</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"eposter-3\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Eposter Abstract 3</p><h3>Laparoscopic buccal mucosa graft ureteroplasty for proximal ureteral stricture: a case report</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Kamal Sachdeva, Dr Swapan Sood</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">PATEL HOSPITAL</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aim - A case report of Laparoscopic buccal mucosa graft ureteroplasty for proximal ureteral stricture</p><p>Methods- We performed Laparoscopic buccal mucosa graft ureteroplasty for 3 cm proximal ureteral stricture over a ureter stent and after 6 weeks ureter stent was removed. We have demonstrated steps of procedure in our presentation.</p><p>Results- Following surgery, Complete resolution of symptoms were noted and radiologically obstruction got relieved. After 3 months DTPA reported GFR improved to 32 ml/min/m2 from 17.5 ml/min/m2</p><p>Conclusion- Lap augmentation ureteroplasty using Buccal mucosa does not hamper ureteral vascularity. It is safe, reproducible &amp; effective solution for long, complex proximal ureteric strictures.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"eposter-4\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Eposter Abstract 4</p><h3>Case Report: Primary Renal Synovial Sarcoma</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr. Amit Kumar kashyap, Dr. Nachiket Vyas, Dr. Manoj Bamaniya, Dr. Sanjeev Jaiswal, Dr. Ramdayal sahu</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">SMS MEDICAL COLLEGE</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>This case report presents a comprehensive review of primary renal synovial sarcoma (PRSS), including its clinical presentation, radiological features, histopathological characteristics, molecular diagnostics, and treatment approaches. Primary renal synovial sarcoma (PRSS) is a rare malignant neoplasm of the kidney that poses diagnostic and therapeutic challenges. Less than 200 cases have been reported. PRSS mostly affects young adults and is associated with poor prognosis. Diagnosis requires a combination of histopathology, immunohistochemistry, and molecular confirmation of the characteristic SYT-SSX fusion gene. Complete surgical resection remains the cornerstone of treatment, with adjuvant chemotherapy used in selected cases only.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"eposter-5\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Eposter Abstract 5</p><h3>Postpubertal Teratoma Arising in an Undescended Inguinal Testis: A Rare Case</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">DR DEBASHISH PANI</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">SMS- JAIPUR</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Objective:<br/>\nTo report the clinical presentation, diagnostic work-up, histopathological findings, and management of a postpubertal teratoma arising in an untreated undescended inguinal testis.</p><p>Materials and Methods:<br/>\nA 38-year-old man presented with a progressively enlarging painful left inguinoscrotal swelling of two months' duration and absent left testis since birth. Clinical examination, serum tumour markers, ultrasonography, and contrast-enhanced computed tomography of the thorax and abdomen were performed. The patient underwent radical inguinal orchidectomy, and the diagnosis was confirmed on histopathological examination.</p><p>Results:<br/>\nClinical examination demonstrated a 9 × 5 cm hard left inguinoscrotal mass with an empty ipsilateral hemiscrotum. Serum alpha-fetoprotein was markedly elevated (&gt;2000 ng/mL), β-human chorionic gonadotropin was 1.2 mIU/mL, and lactate dehydrogenase was 782 U/L. Imaging localized the tumour to the undescended inguinal testis without evidence of distant metastasis. Histopathological examination revealed postpubertal teratoma. The patient was subsequently evaluated for stage-directed management with serial tumour marker assessment and multidisciplinary oncological follow-up.</p><p>Conclusion:<br/>\nPostpubertal teratoma arising in an undescended testis is an uncommon presentation of testicular germ cell tumour. Long-standing cryptorchidism remains a major risk factor for malignant transformation. Persistent inguinal masses associated with an absent ipsilateral testis warrant prompt evaluation, radical inguinal orchidectomy, and appropriate oncological staging. Early diagnosis and multidisciplinary management are essential for favourable outcomes.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"eposter-6\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Eposter Abstract 6</p><h3>Young male patient with pelvic cyst under evaluation</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr.Rahul Parmar,</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">SMS MEDICAL COLLEGE</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>24 Year male presented to SMS UROLOGY OPD with chief complaints of mild coliky pain in abdomen in Left iliac fossa and daily 2 episode of non projectile non bilious vomiting for 1 year. . Patient also complained of anorexia with wt loss 8 kg in the last 1 yr. Patient also complained of dysuria. No other urinary complaints, any fever or any other symptoms. Patient was operated for penoscrotal hypospadias in SMS. 1st stage was done on 10/12/2005. 2nd stage( Byer’s flap Urethroplasty) was done on 9/4/13. No other comorbidities or any substance abuse,Exploratory laparotomy and mullerian cyst excision and marsupialization of cyst wall done.intra op - on cystoscopy- Post urethra- an opening communicating into cystic cavity present in midline just proximal to Verumontanum<br/>\nDuring Laparotomy- 10×5 cm cystic swelling identified at posterior aspect of urinary bladder over right side, originating from prostatic urethra.and post op biopsy suggestive of mullerian cyst</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"eposter-7\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Eposter Abstract 7</p><h3>A Silent Urinary Tract Anomaly in Primary Amenorrhea: Bilateral Ectopic Ureters Discovered After Vaginoplasty</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr sahil middha, Dr devanshu bansal , Dr sanjay mittal , Dr yoge dra singh chundawat</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Sdmh jaipur</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aims<br/>\nTo highlight an unusual and clinically silent urinary tract anomaly associated with primary amenorrhea<br/>\nand emphasize the importance of comprehensive genitourinary evaluation before reconstructive<br/>\nsurgery.<br/>\nMethods<br/>\nA 16-year-old girl with primary amenorrhea and no urinary or gastrointestinal complaints underwent<br/>\nevaluation for genital tract anomaly. Magnetic resonance imaging demonstrated a small contracted<br/>\nleft kidney with marked hydroureter and a grossly dilated right ureter. Diagnostic laparoscopy,<br/>\nvaginoplasty and hysteroscopy were performed. Postoperative urinary output was closely monitored.<br/>\nResults<br/>\nFollowing vaginoplasty, the patient developed anuria, prompting further evaluation. Bilateral ectopic<br/>\nureteric openings were identified in the reconstructed vaginal canal. The left renal unit was atrophic<br/>\nand poorly functioning and was managed with laparoscopic simple nephrectomy. The right ureter was<br/>\nreimplanted into the bladder with placement of a ureteric stent. The patient subsequently recovered<br/>\nwith restoration of urinary drainage.<br/>\nConclusions<br/>\nBilateral ectopic ureters may remain clinically silent and can be overlooked during evaluation of<br/>\nprimary amenorrhea. Unrecognized ectopic ureteric insertion can have serious consequences<br/>\nfollowing vaginoplasty. Detailed preoperative assessment of the urinary tract, including appropriate<br/>\ncross-sectional imaging, is essential in patients with Müllerian/genital tract anomalies. Early<br/>\nrecognition permits coordinated urological and reconstructive management and may prevent<br/>\npostoperative urinary complications.<br/>\nConflict of Interest: No conflict of interest to disclose.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"eposter-8\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Eposter Abstract 8</p><h3>“A Silent Urinary Tract Anomaly in Primary Amenorrhea: Bilateral Ectopic Ureters Discovered After Vaginoplasty”</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr sahil middha, Dr devanshu bansal dr sanjay mittal , dr Yogendra singh chundawat</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Sdmh jaipur</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Introduction:<br/>\nEctopic ureter is an uncommon congenital anomaly of the urinary tract, and bilateral ectopic ureters with<br/>\nvaginal insertion are particularly rare. Their presentation may be delayed, especially when associated<br/>\nwith an abnormal or poorly functioning renal unit. We report an unusual case of bilateral vaginal ectopic<br/>\nureters discovered following gynecological surgery in an adolescent presenting with primary<br/>\namenorrhea.<br/>\nCase Presentation:<br/>\nA 16-year-old girl presented to the gynecology department with primary amenorrhea and no urinary or<br/>\ngastrointestinal complaints. MRI revealed a small contracted left kidney with marked hydroureter and a<br/>\ngrossly dilated contralateral ureter. She underwent diagnostic laparoscopy, vaginal<br/>\ndilatation/vaginoplasty and hysteroscopy. Postoperatively, absence of urine output prompted urological<br/>\nevaluation. Cystoscopy and hysteroscopic assessment demonstrated bilateral ectopic ureteric openings<br/>\ninto the vagina. Further evaluation confirmed a severely atrophic, poorly functioning left renal unit with a<br/>\nmarkedly dilated ureter and a significantly dilated right ureter.<br/>\nManagement:<br/>\nThe patient underwent laparoscopic left simple nephrectomy for the nonfunctioning contracted renal<br/>\nunit. The right ureter was identified, mobilized and reimplanted into the bladder. A ureteric stent was<br/>\nplaced to ensure adequate drainage.<br/>\nConclusion:<br/>\nBilateral vaginal ectopic ureters may remain clinically silent despite significant upper urinary tract<br/>\nabnormalities. In adolescents presenting with primary amenorrhea and genital tract anomalies,<br/>\nassociated urinary tract abnormalities should be actively evaluated. Postoperative anuria in this case<br/>\nserved as an important diagnostic clue to an otherwise occult bilateral ectopic ureteric anomaly. Early<br/>\nmultidisciplinary assessment is essential to preserve the functioning renal unit and prevent progressive<br/>\nrenal damage.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"eposter-9\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Eposter Abstract 9</p><h3>Dorsal urethrocutaneous fistula with iatrogenic hypospadias 15 years after Mitchell epispadias repair: single-stage combined reconstruction</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Naveen Bajiya, Dr Shivam Priyadarshi, Dr Govind Sharma, Dr Sarthak Sharma, Dr Mohit Sethi</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">SMS Medical College</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aims<br/>\nTo report single-stage repair of a long-standing dorsal urethrocutaneous fistula with iatrogenic hypospadias following childhood Mitchell epispadias repair, and to show that apparent “recurrent epispadias” usually represents these two correctable sequelae.<br/>\nMethods<br/>\nAn 18-year-old male who underwent modified Mitchell repair for epispadias at 3 years of age (2010) presented with a dorsal urinary leak noticed one year after surgery and left untreated for 15 years. Examination, uroflowmetry, RGU and operative records were reviewed.<br/>\nResults<br/>\nExamination showed a 3 cm dorsal shaft opening, a ventral subcoronal hypospadiac meatus, ventral hooded prepuce and normal testes, without chordee, infection or lower urinary tract symptoms. Uroflowmetry showed Qmax 29 mL/s with a voided volume of 176 mL; urine was sterile and renal function normal. The penis was degloved through a circumcoronal incision. The dorsal fistula was excised and closed with PDS 5-0 with a dartos second layer. Ventrally, the urethral plate was incised in the midline distal to the meatus, a preputial skin inlay graft was sutured into the defect, and the plate was tubularised over a 10 Fr catheter with subcuticular PDS 5-0, followed by a dartos flap and glanuloplasty. A corrugated drain was removed on day 2 and the patient was discharged on day 6 with the catheter in situ. [Catheter was removed at 2 weeks; at 2 months follow-up he voids from a glanular meatus with no fistula recurrence.]<br/>\nConclusions<br/>\nAfter penile disassembly for epispadias, urethral plate shortening produces a hypospadiac meatus, and dorsal fistula is the commonest late complication; together they are often mislabelled as recurrence. Both can be corrected in one sitting through a single degloving, combining dorsal fistula closure with ventral inlay-graft tubularised urethroplasty. Dartos interposition on both aspects is essential to prevent re-fistulisation. Follow-up into adulthood is warranted, as neglected sequelae may present years later.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"eposter-10\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Eposter Abstract 10</p><h3>Complete androgen insensitivity syndrome (46,XY DSD) presenting as primary amenorrhoea with bilateral inguinal testes</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Naveen Bajiya, Dr Shivam Priyadarshi, Dr Govind Sharma, Dr Abhishek Jain, Dr Mohit Sethi</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">SMS Medical College</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aims<br/>\nTo report a case of complete androgen insensitivity syndrome (CAIS) presenting in adulthood as primary amenorrhoea with bilateral inguinal swellings, and to describe its diagnostic pitfalls, gonadal histopathology and management.<br/>\nMethods<br/>\nCase report of a 28-year-old phenotypic female evaluated at a tertiary care urology centre. Clinical history, examination, hormonal profile, karyotyping, ultrasonography and pelvic MRI were reviewed. She underwent multidisciplinary counselling followed by bilateral gonadectomy, with histopathological examination of both specimens.<br/>\nResults<br/>\nA 28-year-old married phenotypic female presented during infertility evaluation with primary amenorrhoea and bilateral inguinal swellings since birth. Examination revealed normal female genitalia, normal breasts, absent axillary and pubic hair, a short blind-ending vagina without cervix, and bilateral reducible inguinal gonads. Serum testosterone was 136.7 ng/dL, FSH 94.57 mIU/mL, LH 73.77 mIU/mL, oestradiol 24.9 pg/mL, AMH 21.79 ng/mL, with normal DHEA-S. Karyotype was 46,XY. Ultrasonography demonstrated absent uterus and ovaries. Initial whole-abdomen MRI misinterpreted the gonads as small ovaries; dedicated pelvic MRI identified bilateral inguinal testes (right 23×12 mm, left 23×18 mm). After counselling regarding infertility and gonadal malignancy risk, bilateral gonadectomy was performed. Both specimens showed multinodular solid areas and clear-fluid cysts with attached spermatic cords. Microscopy demonstrated atrophic seminiferous tubules with thickened basement membranes, peritubular fibrosis, Leydig cell hyperplasia and chronic peritesticular inflammatory infiltration. Recovery was uneventful and oestrogen replacement was initiated.<br/>\nConclusions<br/>\nCAIS should be considered in any phenotypic female with primary amenorrhoea and bilateral inguinal swellings. The combination of 46,XY karyotype, elevated gonadotrophins and absent Müllerian structures is diagnostic. Dedicated pelvic imaging is essential, as non-targeted studies may misidentify inguinal testes as ovaries. Gonadal histology mirrors the endocrine profile, tubular atrophy and fibrosis accompanying raised FSH and Leydig cell hyperplasia accompanying raised LH. Post-pubertal gonadectomy with histopathological assessment, lifelong oestrogen replacement, genetic counselling and psychological support form the cornerstone of management.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"eposter-11\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Eposter Abstract 11</p><h3>Large introitus mass managed by excision with urethral repair and Martius flap Reconstruction</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr. Ashish Garg</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">SMS HOSPITAL JAIPUR</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Background:<br/>\nLarge introitus and periurethral masses in young females are uncommon and require careful<br/>\nsurgical planning because of their close anatomical relationship with the urethra, anterior<br/>\nvaginal wall and surrounding genital structures.<br/>\nCase Presentation:<br/>\nA 27-year-old female presented with a gradually progressive mass protruding from the<br/>\nintroitus for 5–6 years. She had no significant voiding complaints. Local examination<br/>\nrevealed a well-defined, smooth, protruding mass arising from the introitus/periurethral<br/>\nregion. The mass was clinically close to the urethra, with the rectal wall free on examination.<br/>\nRadiological Findings:<br/>\nPelvic imaging suggested a well-circumscribed solid mass of approximately 6 cm in the<br/>\nintroitus/periurethral region, closely related to the anterior vaginal wall and urethra. The<br/>\nlesion appeared localized without deeper pelvic extension.<br/>\nManagement/Operative Findings:<br/>\nThe patient underwent excision of the introitus mass under anaesthesia. An elliptical incision<br/>\nwas made over the mass, followed by careful submucosal dissection. Intraoperatively, the<br/>\nmass was found adherent to the urethra. It was dissected carefully and excised in toto.<br/>\nUrethral repair was performed using absorbable sutures. A Martius flap was harvested from<br/>\nthe left labial region and placed over the repaired urethra for reinforcement. Vaginal packing<br/>\nwas done after completing the repair. Grossly, the excised specimen was a well<br/>\ncircumscribed, solid, oval mass measuring approximately 6 cm.<br/>\nOutcome:<br/>\nComplete excision of the mass was achieved with urethral repair and Martius flap<br/>\nreconstruction. The specimen was sent for histopathological examination.<br/>\nConclusion:<br/>\nThis case demonstrates the surgical management of a large introitus/periurethral mass in a<br/>\nyoung female. The close adherence of the lesion to the urethra made the procedure<br/>\ntechnically challenging and required urethral repair with Martius flap reconstruction. Careful<br/>\ndissection, complete excision and vascularized flap support are key steps in achieving a safe<br/>\nfunctional outcome.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"eposter-12\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Eposter Abstract 12</p><h3>Second-stage superficialization and transposition of a mature brachiobasilic arteriovenous fistula: a step-by-step surgical technique</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr S Middha, Dr D Bansal , Dr S Mittal , Dr y chundawat</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Sdmh jaipur</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aims:<br/>\nTo describe the surgical technique and early outcome of second-stage superficialization and transposition of a mature brachiobasilic arteriovenous fistula to facilitate hemodialysis access.<br/>\nMethods:<br/>\nA 23-year-old female with chronic kidney disease stage V underwent second-stage surgery for a previously created mature left brachiobasilic arteriovenous fistula. A longitudinal medial arm incision was made over the basilic vein. The arterialized basilic vein was identified and circumferentially dissected with preservation of its vascular integrity. Side branches were individually ligated and divided. The vein was adequately mobilized to obtain sufficient length for tension-free transposition. The basilic vein was divided and re-anastomosed to the brachial artery. A subcutaneous tunnel was created, and the arterialized basilic vein was transposed into a superficial and accessible position. Patency and satisfactory thrill were confirmed before wound closure.<br/>\nResults:<br/>\nThe procedure was completed successfully with minimal blood loss and no significant intraoperative complication. The transposed basilic vein was positioned without tension or kinking, with satisfactory postoperative thrill. The patient had an uneventful postoperative recovery and subsequently underwent successful cannulation of the superficialized fistula for hemodialysis.<br/>\nConclusions:<br/>\nSecond-stage superficialization and transposition of a mature brachiobasilic arteriovenous fistula can effectively convert a deeply situated arterialized basilic vein into an accessible hemodialysis conduit. Careful circumferential dissection, meticulous branch control, adequate mobilization and tension-free superficial positioning are important technical principles. This case demonstrates the procedure step-by-step and highlights practical considerations for achieving a functional and readily cannulatable vascular access.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"eposter-13\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Eposter Abstract 13</p><h3>Submucosal Hematoma Masquerading as Urinary Bladder Mass with Hematuria in a Patient with Immune Thrombocytopenic Purpura</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Shivam Upadhayaya</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">AIIMS RISHIKESH</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Introduction and Aims</p><p>Bladder wall thickening with an endoluminal mass is commonly considered suspicious for urothelial malignancy. However, submucosal hematoma can closely mimic a bladder tumour, particularly in patients with bleeding disorders. We report a case of a bladder mass-like lesion in severe immune thrombocytopenic purpura (ITP).</p><p>Methods</p><p>A 50-year-old man with type 2 diabetes mellitus presented with painless gross hematuria for two months and severe thrombocytopenia, with a platelet nadir of 1,000/µL. Following platelet transfusions, intravenous immunoglobulin and corticosteroids, his platelet count improved to 511 × 10³/µL. CT urography showed heterogeneous circumferential bladder wall thickening with non-enhancing, heterodense, broad-based endoluminal lesions involving the trigone and bilateral vesicoureteric junctions, with left hydroureteronephrosis. After hematological optimisation, cystoscopy and TURBT were performed.</p><p>### Results</p><p>Imaging was initially suggestive of bladder neoplasia. Cystoscopy revealed multiple broad-based lesions involving the lateral walls and dome, with an appearance suggestive of organised hematoma rather than papillary tumour. Complete TURBT was performed with adequate haemostasis. Histopathology showed hyperplastic urothelium with reactive atypia, dense chronic inflammation, hemosiderin-laden macrophages, extensive haemorrhage and necrosis, with no dysplasia or malignancy. The postoperative course was uneventful.</p><p>### Conclusion</p><p>Submucosal bladder hematoma should be considered in patients with hematuria, bladder mass-like lesions and bleeding disorders such as ITP. Clinical, radiological, endoscopic and histopathological correlation is essential to differentiate hemorrhagic lesions from urothelial malignancy. Endoscopic intervention should preferably be performed after adequate hematological optimisation.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"eposter-14\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Eposter Abstract 14</p><h3>Postoperative Outcomes and Early Complications Following Radical Cystectomy with Ileal Conduit at a Tertiary Care Centre: A Prospective Observational Study</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr D Jindal</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">SMS Medical College Jaipur</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aims: To determine the incidence, pattern and severity of complications occurring within 90 days of radical cystectomy.</p><p>Methods: This Prospective Observational Analytical study was conducted over six months in the Department of Urology, SMS Medical College and Attached Hospitals, Jaipur. Eleven adult patients undergoing open radical cystectomy for muscle-invasive or high-risk non-muscle-invasive bladder cancer were enrolled consecutively in the study. Demographic characteristics, clinical profile, operative details and postoperative outcomes were recorded. Complications occurring within 90 days were graded according to the Clavien–Dindo classification. Grade III or higher complications were considered major morbidity. Hospital stay; ICU requirement, re-exploration, readmission and mortality were also assessed.</p><p>Results:<br/>\nThe mean age of the patients was 62.6 ± 7.5 years, and 10 (90.9%) were men. Muscle-invasive bladder cancer was the indication for surgery in 10 (90.9%) patients. All patients underwent open radical cystectomy with bilateral pelvic lymph-node dissection and ileal conduit urinary diversion. Six patients developed postoperative complications, the severity and pattern of which are presented in Figure-1. The mean postoperative hospital stay was 13.1 ± 4.3 days. One patient (9.1%) required readmission within 90 days. No postoperative mortality was observed.</p><p>Conclusions: Radical cystectomy was associated with considerable early morbidity, although most complications were minor and managed conservatively. The occurrence of major morbidity in nearly one-fifth of patients highlights the need for careful patient selection, preoperative optimisation and structured postoperative surveillance. Prompt recognition and management of infectious, gastrointestinal and urinary complications may reduce their severity and improve recovery after radical cystectomy.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"eposter-15\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Eposter Abstract 15</p><h3>Life-saving nephrectomy for emphysematous pyelonephritis in a patient with autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease: a multidisciplinary surgical rescue</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Digvijay Singh Tanwar, Dr Shiv Charan Navriya, Dr Siddharth Garg</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">AIIMS, Jodhpur</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aim: To report a rare case of emphysematous pyelonephritis complicating autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease, and to describe the multidisciplinary decision-making that led to a life-saving emergency nephrectomy after failed percutaneous drainage.</p><p>Methods: A 39-year-old male with autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease and stage V chronic kidney disease on maintenance haemodialysis presented with gross hematuria, fever, and dysuria for ten days, rapidly progressing to septic shock requiring intubation and intensive care. Non-contrast CT of the kidneys, ureters, and bladder showed right-sided emphysematous pyelonephritis with bilateral massively enlarged polycystic kidneys, and urine culture grew Escherichia coli. A 12-Fr right percutaneous nephrostomy was placed, yielding purulent material, alongside broad-spectrum antibiotics, haemodialysis, and intensive care support.</p><p>Results: Despite percutaneous drainage and maximal medical therapy, the patient continued to deteriorate with persistent septic shock and escalating vasopressor requirements. An emergency open right simple nephrectomy was performed, revealing a massively enlarged (25 x 15 cm) necrotic, infected kidney with dense adhesions to the liver and posterior abdominal wall. The postoperative course was complicated by respiratory failure, persistent sepsis, and burst abdomen on postoperative day 15, managed with secondary suturing. With coordinated urology, nephrology, and critical care input, the patient improved and was discharged in a hemodynamically stable condition on maintenance haemodialysis.</p><p>Conclusions: Emphysematous pyelonephritis complicating autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease is rare and carries high mortality, even in non-diabetic patients. The multicystic renal architecture limits both antibiotic penetration and percutaneous source control, so failure of drainage should prompt early consideration of nephrectomy despite the technical difficulty of operating on a massively enlarged, adherent kidney. Coordinated multidisciplinary care involving urology, nephrology, and critical care is essential, and this case demonstrates that survival is achievable even after a prolonged, complicated postoperative course.</p><p>Conflict of Interest: No conflict of interest to disclose.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"eposter-16\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Eposter Abstract 16</p><h3>En-bloc dual adult kidney transplantation with multiple vessels in a short-stature recipient</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Saurabh Girotra, Dr Abhiyutthan Singh Jadaon</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">ILBS, NEW DELHI</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Introduction: We describe an en-bloc technique of transplanting two adult kidneys into a single iliac fossa using the donor aorta and inferior vena cava for revascularization.</p><p>Materials and Methods: A 28-year-old man, 146 cm tall, with end-stage renal disease on hemodialysis for 12 years underwent dual kidney transplantation from a 60-year-old diabetic deceased donor with a terminal creatinine of 1.4 mg/dL. The kidneys were retrieved en bloc and had two left renal arteries, one right renal artery, one left renal vein, and two right renal veins. The lower ends of the donor aorta and inferior vena cava were anastomosed to the right common iliac artery and external iliac vein, respectively. The kidneys were placed one above the other in the right iliac fossa. The two ureters were implanted as a common stump using an onlay technique over two DJ stents. Abdominal closure was facilitated by an external oblique release incision.</p><p>Results: Recipient operative time, including bench preparation, was 200 minutes. The patient had ATN for 5 days, followed by good urine output. Serum creatinine was 0.8 mg/dL on postoperative day 12 at discharge and 0.7 mg/dL at 4 months. Both DJ stents were removed on day 30. One episode of urinary tract infection occurred at 2 months and was treated with oral antibiotics. There was no vascular thrombosis, kinking, reduced blood flow, or urinary drainage complication. Ultrasound at 3 months showed both kidneys in the right iliac fossa without hydronephrosis, with good PSV and RI of 0.6. There was no proteinuria at 4 months.</p><p>Conclusion: En-bloc transplantation of two adult kidneys into a single iliac fossa is feasible using a single arterial and venous anastomosis. It avoids dissection of both iliac fossae and may reduce recipient operative and cold ischemia times.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"eposter-17\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Eposter Abstract 17</p><h3>GIANT PURE VIRILIZING ADRENAL CORTICAL CARCINOMA IN YOUNG ADULT</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Aisha Fatma, Dr Manoj Talwar, Dr Dipti Kalita</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">batra hospital</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Introduction<br/>\nAdrenocortical carcinoma (ACC) is a rare and aggressive endocrine malignancy with an incidence of 0.5–2 cases per million annually. Although many ACCs are hormonally functional, pure androgen-secreting tumors in adults are exceedingly uncommon and may present with subtle or nonspecific clinical manifestations, often leading to delayed diagnosis.</p><p>Case Presentation<br/>\nA 29-year-old female presented with secondary amenorrhea for 18 months and vague right upper abdominal discomfort. Ultrasonography performed during gynaecological evaluation incidentally revealed a large right suprarenal mass. Contrast-enhanced computed tomography demonstrated a huge heterogeneous adrenal lesion with displacement of adjacent structures without any invasion or metastasis. Hormonal evaluation revealed elevated dehydroepiandrosterone levels, while other endocrine parameters were within normal limits. PET-CT confirmed an FDG-avid lesion localized to the adrenal gland. The patient underwent open right adrenalectomy with uneventful peri-operative course. Histopathological examination confirmed adrenocortical carcinoma with a low mitotic index and no capsular or vascular invasion.</p><p>Discussion<br/>\nPure virilizing ACC in adults is extremely rare. Due to nonspecific clinical features, diagnosis may be delayed.Increased awareness high suspicion ,Imaging and hormonal evaluation are essential for early detection as complete surgical resection remains the cornerstone of treatment and offers the best chance for favorable outcomes.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"eposter-18\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Eposter Abstract 18</p><h3>Papillary renal cell carcinoma with extensive osseous metaplasia: A rare pathological entity</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Aisha Fatma, Dr Manoj Talwar, Dr Dipti Kalita</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">batra hospital</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Introduction<br/>\nCalcification is frequently encountered radiologically in various subtypes of renal cell carcinoma (RCC). In contrast, metaplastic bone formation, with or without bone marrow elements, is exceedingly rare, with fewer than 50 cases reported across the major RCC subtypes and only two previously reported cases in papillary RCC. We present a rare case of papillary RCC with extensive osseous metaplasia.<br/>\nCase Report<br/>\nA 78-year-old man presented with left flank pain for five months, without hematuria, palpable mass, or constitutional symptoms. Contrast-enhanced computed tomography demonstrated a densely calcified exophytic lesion measuring 3 × 3 × 3.5 cm with an associated soft-tissue component. The patient underwent left partial nephrectomy. Gross examination revealed an extremely hard exophytic lesion. Histopathological examination of the soft-tissue component revealed papillary renal cell carcinoma, while the densely calcified component demonstrated extensive osseous metaplasia. The postoperative course was uneventful, and the patient was doing well at three-month follow-up.<br/>\nDiscussion<br/>\nThe pathogenesis of osseous metaplasia in RCC remains unclear. Proposed mechanisms include osteoblastic metaplasia of tumor or stromal cells, possibly mediated by bone morphogenetic proteins such as BMP-2, and secondary changes related to ischemia, necrosis, hemorrhage, and fibrosis. Most reported cases have been low-grade, organ-confined tumors without metastasis or subsequent progression, suggesting a potentially favorable prognosis. However, osseous metaplasia must be distinguished from sarcomatoid carcinoma with osteosarcomatous differentiation, which carries a poor prognosis. Papillary RCC with extensive osseous metaplasia remains exceptionally rare, and further studies are required to clarify its biological and prognostic significance.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"eposter-19\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Eposter Abstract 19</p><h3>A Difficult Pelvic Giant to Tame: Undifferentiated Pleomorphic Sarcoma of the Prostate Masquerading as Benign Prostatic Hyperplasia—Successful En-bloc Resection with Segmental Pubectomy</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Pankaj gupta, Dr Aditya Pradhan, Dr Rakshit pancholi, Dr Ashutosh Kumar</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Venkateshwar superspeciality hospital</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aims<br/>\nUndifferentiated pleomorphic sarcoma (UPS) of the prostate is an exceptionally rare and aggressive mesenchymal malignancy. Its nonspecific lower urinary tract symptoms and often normal prostate-specific antigen (PSA) can lead to misdiagnosis as (BPH), delaying definitive treatment. Complete surgical excision with negative margins remains the cornerstone of management.<br/>\nMethod<br/>\nA 69-year-old man presented with progressive LUTS recurrent urinary retention, constipation, weight loss. He had undergone two TURP elsewhere for presumed BPH, with benign HPE ; however, symptoms recurred within two months. Examination revealed a large pelvic mass extending to the umbilicus and a hard, markedly enlarged prostate on DRE with preserved rectal mucosa. PSA was 3 ng/mL. MRI ,FDG PET-CT demonstrated a large heterogeneous prostatic mass with extensive necrosis, compressing the bladder and rectum, without distant metastases. TRUS-guided biopsy with IHC revealed a high-grade prostatic stromal sarcoma.</p><p>The patient underwent radical en-bloc resection. Posterior dissection from the rectum was achieved; however, dense anterior fixation precluded safe access to the (DVC), prostatic apex, and proximal urethra. With orthopedic assistance, segmental pubectomy was performed to obtain adequate surgical exposure. The tumour was subsequently excised en bloc with cystectomy while preserving the rectum, followed by ileal conduit urinary diversion and pubic reconstruction.</p><p>Results<br/>\nFinal histopathology demonstrated undifferentiated pleomorphic sarcoma, FNCLCC Grade 3, with 70% tumour necrosis and &gt;20 mitoses/10 high-power fields. Surgical margins were negative, with pathological stage pT4N0. The postoperative course was uneventful.</p><p>Conclusion<br/>\nProstatic UPS should be considered in patients with rapidly recurrent “BPH,” disproportionate prostate enlargement, normal PSA, systemic symptoms, or atypical clinical findings. Early cross-sectional imaging and tissue diagnosis are essential. In selected patients with locally advanced disease, radical en-bloc resection achieving R0 margins offers the best opportunity for local disease control. Segmental pubectomy can provide critical anterior pelvic access for safe resection of giant locally advanced prostatic sarcomas</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"eposter-20\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Eposter Abstract 20</p><h3>MRI-Confirmed Right Lateral Penile Fracture Following Anal Intercourse: A Case Report with Radiological–Operative Correlation</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr S Middha , Dr S Mittal , Dr D Bansal , Dr D dev , Dr Y chundawat</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Sdmh jaipur</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aims:<br/>\nTo report an unusual case of penile fracture following anal intercourse and to highlight the role of magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) in accurately localizing the tunical defect and its correlation with intraoperative findings.<br/>\nMethods:<br/>\nA 33-year-old male presented to the emergency department approximately 7 hours after penile trauma sustained during anal intercourse. The patient reported an audible cracking sound followed by immediate detumescence and progressive penile swelling. There was no hematuria or voiding difficulty. MRI of the penis was performed for anatomical localization of the injury. Emergency surgical exploration was subsequently undertaken under spinal anesthesia. A 14-Fr silicone Foley catheter was placed, followed by circumcoronal incision and penile degloving. The intraoperative findings were documented and compared with the MRI findings.<br/>\nResults:<br/>\nMRI demonstrated a 4-mm focal breach involving the lateral aspect of the proximal right corpus cavernosum with an adjacent hematoma measuring approximately 2.2 × 3.8 cm. Diffuse penile shaft edema extended to the glans, with associated edema of the penile root, right scrotal wall, and inguinal region. The urethra and corpus spongiosum were intact.<br/>\nSurgical exploration revealed breach of Buck's fascia with approximately 35 mL of hematoma. A 1.5-cm longitudinal tear was identified on the right lateral aspect of the proximal corpus cavernosum, corresponding precisely to the MRI-localized defect. The tunical tear was repaired using interrupted 3-0 Vicryl sutures. No urethral injury was identified. The patient tolerated the procedure without intraoperative complications.<br/>\nConclusions:<br/>\nPenile fracture is a urological emergency requiring prompt diagnosis and surgical management. Although the diagnosis is predominantly clinical, MRI can provide accurate anatomical localization of the tunical defect and associated hematoma in selected cases. This case demonstrates excellent radiological–operative correlation and highlights the usefulness of MRI in preoperative localization of penile fracture.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"eposter-21\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Eposter Abstract 21</p><h3>Foreign Body Retrieval Using an Engage® Stone Basket During Bronchoscopy in a 12-Month-Old Infant: A Novel Cross-Specialty Application</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Sagar Sawant , Dr Omang Agarwal, Dr Shailendra Goel</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Fortis Hospital Noida</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Background: Foreign body aspiration (FBA) is a common and potentially fatal emergency in infants. When conventional bronchoscopic retrieval tools fail, clinicians must adapt alternative methods.<br/>\nCase Presentation: We report the successful bronchoscopic removal of an impacted organic airway foreign body in a 12-month-old infant using an Engage® nitinol stone retrieval basket—an instrument traditionally used in retrograde intrarenal surgery (RIRS). Conventional forceps repeatedly failed to grasp the slippery, friable pistachio fragment. The Engage basket was passed through the bronchoscope channel, deployed beyond the object, and used to ensnare and extract it atraumatically. The infant recovered uneventfully.<br/>\nConclusion: This innovative adaptation demonstrates the utility of urological retrieval devices in paediatric bronchoscopy when standard instruments are ineffective. Cross-disciplinary borrowing of tools can improve safety and expand therapeutic options in challenging airway scenarios.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"eposter-22\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Eposter Abstract 22</p><h3>Perihepatic &amp; Right Perirenal Hematoma Following Air Travel after Robotic-Assisted Right Partial Nephrectomy</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Sagar Sawant , Dr Omang Agarwal, Dr Shailendra Goel</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Fortis Hospital Noida</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Background: Robotic-assisted partial nephrectomy (RAPN) is an established nephron-sparing procedure with a low incidence of major postoperative complications. Delayed hemorrhage is uncommon, and hematoma expansion related to early postoperative air travel has rarely been reported.¹–³ We present a unique case of perihepatic and right perirenal hematoma occurring after commercial air travel following RAPN.<br/>\nCase Presentation: A 53-year-old female patient underwent uneventful robotic-assisted right partial nephrectomy for a localized renal mass. Postoperative recovery was initially stable, with no evidence of active bleeding, and the patient was discharged in good condition. Five days postoperatively, the patient undertook commercial air travel. Shortly after arrival, the patient developed acute right upper quadrant and flank pain associated with a significant drop in hemoglobin levels. Contrast-enhanced computed tomography of the abdomen revealed a large right perirenal hematoma with extension into the perihepatic (subhepatic) region, without evidence of active contrast extravasation or vascular injury.³,⁴<br/>\nManagement and Outcome: The patient was managed conservatively with hemodynamic monitoring, blood transfusion, analgesia, and bed rest. Serial imaging demonstrated stabilization and gradual resolution of the hematoma. The patient recovered without the need for angioembolization or surgical re-exploration.²,⁴<br/>\nDiscussion: This case highlights the potential role of early postoperative air travel in precipitating or exacerbating delayed hemorrhage after partial nephrectomy. Changes in cabin pressure, hypoxia-induced hemodynamic alterations, dehydration, and mechanical stress may contribute to clot destabilization at the renal resection bed.⁶,⁷ The anatomical continuity of retroperitoneal spaces explains the extension of hematoma into the perihepatic region.⁸<br/>\nConclusion: Perihepatic and perirenal hematoma following early air travel after RAPN is a rare but clinically significant complication. Surgeons should counsel patients to avoid early air travel after nephron-sparing renal surgery and maintain a high index of suspicion for delayed hemorrhage in symptomatic patients.</p></div></details></article>"

